<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">San Juan River (Nicaragua)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The San Juan River flows about 190 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nicaragua">Lake Nicaragua</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, forming much of the border between Nicaragua and Costa Rica. With the lake it offered a natural route across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, guarded by the Spanish fortress of El Castillo, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> gold rush <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornelius-vanderbilt">Cornelius Vanderbilt</a>'s company carried passengers along it. It was long studied as a route for an interoceanic canal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/san-juan-river-nicaragua.jpg" alt="San Juan River (Nicaragua)" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rodrigo Castillo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Riosanjuan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
